menu "espOS log ring"

    config ESPOS_LOG_RING_SIZE
        int "Log ring buffer size (bytes)"
        default 16384
        range 2048 262144
        help
            Recent log lines are kept in RAM for GET /api/v1/logs. Oldest
            lines are overwritten first.

    config ESPOS_LOG_LINE_MAX
        int "Longest stored line (bytes)"
        default 256
        range 64 1024
        help
            Longer lines are truncated when stored (console output is
            unaffected).

    config ESPOS_LOG_WALLCLOCK
        bool "Stamp stored lines with the wall clock once it is known"
        default y
        help
            The IDF log prefix carries milliseconds since boot, which is what
            you want for "how long after startup did this happen" and useless
            for lining a device's log up against a server's. With this on,
            once espos_time has a clock every line stored in the ring carries
            both — the monotonic value stays exactly where it was and the UTC
            time is added beside it:

                I (18452|2026-09-07T10:12:13.456Z) espos_sk: stream connected

            Lines logged before the clock is set are unchanged, so a boot log
            reads as it always did, and so is the console output: only what
            /api/v1/logs serves is stamped. A firmware built without
            espos_time keeps the monotonic-only prefix whatever this is set
            to, because there is no clock to ask.

endmenu
